{"kind":"report","text":"Static reconciliation of externally supplied cloud review inputs, under checker request f4d418f62922da4fcc0a770b84036a7040bd8d8f and promise 4ce41a2a722157aca431fdeacd26f7c169c8c0b7.\n\nExact documents: 22b593c79c9c2c0b2b21c7b3c9eb86e834d18431, notes/cloud-reviews/2026-10-04-layering-review.md (24828 bytes, SHA-256 2405e32a19b1d21574a6d054d0288448f550e474319228b56f218ee4907572ab) and notes/cloud-reviews/2026-10-04-security-review.md (35544 bytes, SHA-256 69343de16103b36d62bb58d8f5dd59a15686c94014bfbcd90cc7a80a2b738e04). Both authors reviewed old main 4a7a13a11313e7c26a21e5735d3d1c0df269b291. Layering explicitly could not read the I1 plan (405–409). Checker read both notes completely, through EOF; 66844 characters of numbered reading. No attached scripts or described commands/tests were executed. Their code/run/inference labels, partial source coverage, Node-only comparisons and absent live provider/container evidence remain attributed. Their statement that no High finding was found is not security certification of old main, current I1 or future deployment.\n\nUseful details to carry into the existing complete follow-through:\n\n1. Invitation anti-abuse needs an explicit successor disposition. The security note's M3 (201–227) identifies an unauthenticated per-invitation budget consumed before signature verification; M4 (229–255) identifies a shared bounded address/invitation window table whose new-window refusal can deny every legitimate new redemption. A parallel static reread at the stated old source confirms admission.ts:237 precedes signature checking at 250, http.ts:132 direct acts has no address input, requests.ts:242 counts an address before admission, and complete ratelimit.ts:1–70 uses one map and refuses new windows at 40. Adopted R1:1567 puts rate limiting in front of directory/membership, and 1953 retains ratelimit.ts; table migration at 1984 and R3's invitation semantics do not yet explicitly resolve these two abuse paths. R1/R3/I3 must decide authentication/secret-check ordering, separate abuse budgets or their chosen alternative, overload behavior and what a valid invitation can still do. Keep transient serving protection distinct from authoritative consumption of a single-use invitation. Do not copy the note's eviction or always-admit remedy without considering how it changes abuse resistance and bounded work. This is a concrete historical detail requiring successor disposition, not a command to repair the parked Room.\n\n2. Member-authored text must remain data at the agent/tool boundary. Security M6 (281–313) covers goals, plans, notes, review text and labels in unmarked MCP output. A parallel static reread confirms the old summary/result construction at run.ts:322,328,377–380 and instructions at tools.ts:584–591. Current R3:742–743 says checker sandbox output is data; that concerns result grammar, not member prose read by a hosted agent. R3:945–956 authenticates controller/admin tool requests but does not prevent read prose influencing the agent's own permitted choices. R3 with I5/IA must explicitly retain authorship/data boundaries in tool results and summaries and keep room prose from becoming host instructions, consent or new authority. Marking and one instruction sentence alone prove no prompt-injection resistance; scoped admission, signer/gateway isolation and control authorization still apply. A note requesting a token or control change grants nothing. Resolve this in the existing interface and agent work, without a parallel policy engine.\n\n3. Name the human revocation policy rather than inheriting the old lease behavior. M1's original claim is that key/member revocation leaves a fetched fork credential and its hold live until lease end; its proposed immediate cancellation is not automatically adopted. Current R3:394–412 expressly chooses freshness windows for new acts, and 1330 ends holds when an agent key is revoked. That agent row does not itself establish the policy for a person's revoked device/member and existing workspace credential. R3 should state the intended human hold/token disposition, whether bounded continued private-fork writes are permitted, and the fences that prevent unauthorized minting, staging or new report. Preserve other active devices where intended. A token's known expiry/revocation never proves an already sent write stopped or clears unknown mint, command, environment or deletion duties.\n\n4. Keep operational trust details in existing source/acceptance owners. L11 (553–564) identifies actual runner image c.images.runner at sandbox.ts:134–136 differing from the image setting included in its digest at 176; a parallel static reread confirms this historical mismatch. Carry actual executable/image identity, check configuration and resolved dependencies into I3 runner provenance and R3/R4 check evidence, including a declared setting versus what ran. L4 and L14 identify command-line credential disclosure and unpinned credential-bearing tooling. I3/I5/IA and operator acceptance should keep secret transport out of process arguments/history, use the pinned reviewed tool, and account for active credentials during replacement. These are successor/retarget details; no old deploy script is to be run or repaired by checker.\n\nAlready owned or superseded concerns, without duplicate commissions:\n\n- M2 and L12/L13: R1:1676–1692 retains actual inputs and reports missing dependencies; 1750–1774 requires source replay or a caller-trusted anchor and bounded scopes/entries/bytes/depth; 1776–1811 reports target, coverage, anchors, trusts and equivocation limits. R3 owns receipt custody. A self-consistent unanchored history is not proof of expected service identity, freshness or non-equivocation. Keep these exact boundaries in the real verifier/CLI; do not label it simply verified. The independent verifier and its real cost remain unfinished.\n- M7: R1 D1-6 at 61 says scopes hold no signing keys. Therefore old permanent room-key derivation/rotation is not a reason to introduce scope signing or another key engine. R3:658's deployment-authenticated read sessions and agent/checker secret-store keys are different trust boundaries; their compromise, rotation and custody remain real I3/R3 duties, not proof that all secrets disappeared.\n- M5 and L8/L9: literal glob exclusion, evaluator parse-depth failures and variable-scope defects belong to removal of old policy semantics and actual R1/R2 profile/guard validation. Do not copy the fragile literal check or add a second evaluator. Default refusal of unsupported profiles is partial closure, not delivery of the promised supported meanings.\n- L1/L2/L3 and Info I1–I5/I8/I10: membership/recovery shape and authority, expired/refused intent versus settlement, discovery versus permission, private secret lifetime and redaction belong to I1/I3/I5 successor admission/custody. Retained public history is distinct from retaining old plaintext secrets. Credential cleanup must preserve unsettled duties; deletion alone is not settlement.\n- L5/L6/L7/L10 and Info I6/I7: authority checks before outside preparation, renewal behavior, unknown token creates, actual whole-job deadlines, cancellation and gateway/environment boundaries remain R3/R4/I3/I5 obligations. Keep every unknown attempt under its identity and validate at actual storage/service/client boundaries. No claim of real provider/container/cancellation evidence follows from the old tests or comments.\n- Repository ignore/action pinning (Info I9), runtime manifests and all old scripts remain whole-project retarget/operator acceptance details. Existing old code being parked is neither repair nor removal proof.\n\nLayering reconciliation: no new unowned material layering defect was established. The old graph/cycle/cast/count claims are historical input, not fresh measurements. Current I1 already assigns separate contract/bytes/derive/runtime/transport/replay/client modules, shared synchronous bytes, one pure judgment model, narrow common libraries, runtime declarations, reviewed reuse and S6 selector/manifest/docs/removal retargeting. The layering note's WebCrypto-only advice conflicts with the chosen synchronous transaction boundary; its independent-verifier-copy suggestion must not create another derivation model. Preserve independent integrity/provenance checking while sharing the adopted pure judgments. Actual contract/bytes/derive configs and manifests, bytes domains/sign/web declarations and derive imports were read statically by the parallel audit; it examined import/export lines across all 28 active source files, not all their bodies or compiler discovery. The small derive fixture imports its barrel at line 15, so three tests share broad reachability; this is an already owned S3/S6/R4 cost watch item before heavier fixtures arrive, not the old 12300-line Room suite, a numerical test quota or a request for another selector. Actual measured whole setup/install/edit-to-review CPU/wall cost and the 10x target remain owed.\n\nCoverage: checker personally read both complete exact notes and used the prior complete adopted R1 v7 (05244cfbcd2619c877cbcfcac5985b47651bb31a), R3 v9 (8b8f946d2b7e2e060f7a34ce76960eeee709448d), R4 v8 (c3e699a68303a7431d4cd00c98257c0844fc997a) and I1 plan/source reads. Pertinent R1:1676–1838,1941–1992 and R3:381–446,721–750,991–1070,1318–1340,1499–1528 were reread in full. Two parallel audits fully read the assigned security/layering note and live design follow-through texts, with the selective historical-source verification described above; neither reran external review scripts or tests. I1 plan SHA-256 433dacca84189bdb339e5f0ed2bd4d68887ce628ffcf211b5f2ea4ebe6fe9e52 transfers byte-identically from 1712fa40 to c05.\n\nAttach the four useful details to the existing R1 1e85696d, R3 1026ed6a and applicable R4 d85e686c follow-through, with source owners I3/I5/IA. Actual choices need named dispositions in the revised existing subjects and later ordinary review. This report creates no new architecture note, engine, heavy suite, Jam gate, provider operation, old-package repair or schedule-based scope cut. The eight current I1 P2 source findings remain separately owned by report d3930ee8 and request 373467f1; this historical-input review neither duplicates nor closes them. Checker executed no project code, tests, builds, installs, typecheck, private diagnostics, cloud-review scripts or provider probes and changed no source."}